course: history
lesson: 16
title: Rome: the fall of the West and what Rome left us
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Emperor Constantine became the first Christian emperor, allowed Christians to worship freely in 313 and founded a new capital, Constantinople, today's Istanbul.
explain: The empire grew too big for one ruler and was split into a western half ruled from Rome and an eastern half ruled from Constantinople. The West had many troubles at once: civil wars, money problems, disease and many peoples pushing across its borders.
explain: Think of a leaky old house: no single drip brings it down, but many leaks together, year after year, do. In 410 the Visigoths looted Rome, and in 476 the last western emperor, a boy, was removed, but for most people life changed slowly.
explain: The East, which we call the Byzantine Empire, lasted another thousand years. If you play Europa Universalis you meet it at the start as a small state, long after its great days. Meanwhile, monks in the West copied old books by hand and kept learning alive.
fact: Constantine became the first Christian emperor, in 313 allowed Christians to worship freely, and founded a new capital, Constantinople, today's Istanbul.
fact: By about 380 Christianity became the official religion of the Roman Empire, and it later spread across Europe.
fact: The empire was split into a western half ruled from Rome and an eastern half ruled from Constantinople.
fact: The western empire faced civil wars, money troubles, disease and many peoples pushing across its borders, such as Goths, Vandals and Huns; many problems together weakened it, not one single cause.
fact: In 410 the Visigoths captured and looted Rome itself, the first time an enemy had taken the city in about 800 years.
fact: Attila led the Huns, horse-riding warriors from the east, and attacked both halves of the empire, and the Huns pushed other peoples into Roman lands.
fact: In 476 a Germanic general removed the last western emperor, a boy named Romulus Augustulus, and this date is often given as the fall of Rome.
fact: Rome did not fall in a day: for most people life changed slowly, and the West broke into kingdoms that later became countries like France, Spain and England.
fact: The eastern empire, which we call the Byzantine Empire, lasted another thousand years until 1453.
fact: The Byzantines kept Roman law and Greek books alive and spread Orthodox Christianity to places like Kyiv and Moscow.
fact: The Byzantine emperor Justinian built the great church of Hagia Sophia with its huge dome and collected Roman law into one book.
fact: Rome left us roads, laws, the alphabet, the names of months, languages, buildings and the Christian church.
fact: After the fall of the West, monks in monasteries copied old books by hand, and without them many Roman and Greek books would be lost.
exercise: Who was the first Christian Roman emperor? | Constantine | Nero | Augustus | Justinian
exercise: What did Constantine allow in 313? | Christians to worship freely | Gladiator fights to end for ever | Vikings to settle in Rome and Milan | Women to join the Senate as members
exercise: What is Constantinople called today? | Istanbul | Athens | Rome | Kyiv
exercise: Into which two halves was the empire split? | A western half and an eastern half | A northern half and a southern half | A Greek half and an Egyptian half | A land half and a sea half
exercise: Why did the western empire grow weak? | Many problems together: wars, money, disease and borders | One single bad emperor who ruined everything by himself | A giant earthquake | The Romans forgot Latin
exercise: Who looted Rome in 410? | The Visigoths | The Vikings | The Persians | The Mongols
exercise: Who led the Huns? | Attila | Hannibal | Spartacus the gladiator | Genghis Khan
exercise: Which date is often given as the fall of the western empire? | 476 | 1453 | 753 BC | 1066
exercise: Who was the last western emperor? | A boy named Romulus Augustulus | Julius Caesar, who came back to rule once more | Constantine | Justinian
exercise: What did the West break into after 476? | Kingdoms that later became countries like France and Spain | One big new empire ruled from Paris by a Roman general | Empty land with no people | Greek city-states
exercise: Until when did the Byzantine Empire last? | Until 1453 | Until the year 476 | Until the year 410 | Until the year 2000
exercise: Which kind of Christianity did the Byzantines spread to Kyiv and Moscow? | Orthodox Christianity | Viking religion | Buddhism | Greek myths
exercise: Which emperor built Hagia Sophia? | Justinian | Constantine | Hadrian | Augustus
exercise: What else did Justinian do? | Collected Roman law into one book | Built Hadrian's Wall across the north of Britain | Invented the alphabet | Defeated Hannibal
exercise: Who kept old books alive in the West? | Monks copying them by hand | Gladiators resting in the arena | Vikings on longships | Printers working with metal machines
exercise: Which of these did Rome leave us? | The alphabet and the names of months | Chocolate, potatoes and tomatoes from America | Paper and gunpowder | Zero and chess
test: a mate says rome fell in one day in 476 and everyone woke up in the dark ages. right? | No, for most people life changed slowly | Yes, everything changed overnight | Yes, and Rome vanished from the map | No, Rome never fell at all
test: My teacher said, no wait, I think she said Rome fell because of one bad emperor. What do historians say? | Many problems together weakened the West | One emperor ruined the whole empire all by himself | It fell because of a volcano | It was sold to Persia
test: In Europa Universalis Byzantium is a tiny state at the start. Why? | By then it was near its end, long after its great days | It was always a tiny state, even under the great Justinian | It was a Viking land | The game made it up
test: If monks had not copied books, what might we have lost? | Many Roman and Greek books | All the Viking runes | The Chinese printing blocks | The Egyptian pyramids
test: The Visigoths took Rome in 410. Why were people so shocked? | Rome had not been taken by an enemy for about 800 years | Rome was attacked and taken by enemies every single week | Nobody lived in Rome | The Visigoths were Roman senators
test: Why did the Huns cause trouble even where they did not go? | They pushed other peoples into Roman lands | They built tall walls everywhere they could find stone | They sailed up every river | They stopped all trade with China
test: People in Kyiv became Orthodox Christians. From whom did that come? | From Byzantium | From the Vikings | From the Aztecs | From China, along the Silk Road
test: Why is it fair to say Rome did not really end in 476? | The eastern half lived on for another thousand years | Rome still had an emperor living in the Colosseum in 2000 | The West grew stronger after 476 | Nobody noticed anything at all
test: Your class uses the Latin alphabet and months like July and August. What does that show? | Much of our way of life rests on Roman foundations | The Romans copied our alphabet and our months from us later | It is a coincidence | English came before Rome
test: An empire is too big for one ruler. What did Rome do? | Split into an eastern and a western half | Gave up all its land and went back to seven hills | Moved everyone to Rome | Elected two hundred emperors
test: Hagia Sophia's huge dome, Roman law in one book. Which emperor? | Justinian | Nero | Constantine | Attila
exam: Identify the emperor who in 313 granted Christians the liberty of worship and founded Constantinople. | Constantine | Justinian | Augustus | Romulus Augustulus
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the decline of the western Roman Empire? | It resulted from numerous concurrent difficulties over a protracted period | It resulted from a single catastrophe in one day | It resulted solely from the conduct of one emperor | It did not occur
exam: Ascertain the year in which the Byzantine Empire, successor to the eastern Roman Empire, came to an end. | 1453 | 476 | 410 | 1066
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the preservation of classical literature in the West. | Monks in monasteries transcribed ancient books by hand | The books were printed by Gutenberg in 500 | The Huns preserved them in libraries | No classical books survive
